Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:02:54 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?feat(g1):=20codebase=20map=20=E2=80=94=20contex?=
 =?UTF-8?q?t=20pack,=20heatmap,=20MCP=20tools,=20drawer=20tab?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

When a user picks a repository in their VM workspace, the app builds a
codebase map for it once and keeps it fresh when the repository moves on.

- lib/codebase: the map builder. A small scanner program runs inside the
  user's VM (the repository lives there), returns the raw material — files,
  sizes, languages, exports, imports, 90-day churn — and the server turns it
  into the context pack (languages, entry points, build/test commands, the
  top-level modules with a one-line purpose each, the key files with their
  exports ranked by import fan-in, the conventions) and into the heatmap
  (churn x size x fan-in x the files this account's agent touched).
  The pack is capped at 8k tokens, estimated and truncated, never more.
- The map is stored as JSON in the VM at ~/.swarm/codebase/<repoHash>/
  (map.json + context-pack.md), so the agent's MCP tools and the app read
  the very same document.
- Every run on the picked repository gets the pack prepended to its system
  prompt, once per run; the run log shows a codebase.context.injected event
  with the token count. Settings -> "Inject codebase context" turns it off.
- Two MCP tools for the agent: codebase_map_get and codebase_hotspots(n).
- UI: a Codebase tab in the right drawer with a squarified treemap of the
  hot files (click one to open it in Files), the repo picker and the pack.
- Tests: unit tests for the ranking, the token cap, the pack builder, the
  scanner program and the MCP tools; an e2e spec for the drawer tab.
